  "explanation": {
    "short": "Before e, i, ae and oe the letter c is soft, like \"ch\" in \"church\", and g is soft, like \"j\" in \"gentle\". So cena is CHEH-nah and magister is mah-JIS-ter. The letters ae are said like \"e\" in \"bed\": caelum is CHEH-loom.",
    "more": "Before a, o, u, or a consonant, c is hard like \"k\" and g is hard like the g in \"go\": clavis is KLAH-vis. The same letters, read in the Italian way, are the Church Latin pronunciation used in the Mass and in the Vulgate."
  },
